  "worked_solution": "First, note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $n=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
